CLINE PROJECT RULES

These rules apply to every task in this workspace, regardless of subject.
They govern how you verify your own work, how you organize files, and how
you hand off when context runs low. The task itself goes in the block
below — everything else on this page stays the same across tasks.

════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
TASK

<< PASTE YOUR TASK PROMPT HERE. Replace this whole block before starting. >>

On starting a new (non-resumed) task, copy this prompt into TASK.md at the
workspace root if TASK.md doesn't already contain it. That gives you a
file to point back to later instead of re-reading this rules file for it.

════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
HARD CONSTRAINTS — apply to every task, no exceptions

1. Never report the result of a check, test, or command you did not
   actually run. If you haven't run something, say "I haven't verified
   this yet" — do not describe what it would probably show.
2. Never guess a fact a tool could confirm: a file's existence, a URL's
   status, today's date, a library's exact export name, a package
   version. If a tool can answer it, call the tool. If no tool can, say
   so explicitly and ask — don't fill the gap from memory or training
   data, which may be stale or wrong for this specific case.
3. Do not declare a task complete until you've verified the result by
   actually running it — executing the code, loading the page, running
   the test suite, whatever "running" means for this task. A plausible-
   looking result is not a verified one.
4. If a requirement is ambiguous, or the task doesn't say what to do in
   a situation you hit, stop and ask rather than picking an
   interpretation and hoping it's right. One clarifying question beats a
   well-built wrong thing.
5. If you get blocked — a tool fails, a fetch 404s, a command errors —
   say exactly what happened and what you tried. Don't quietly
   substitute an unapproved workaround, and don't fabricate to make the
   blocker disappear.

════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
FILE & WORKSPACE CONVENTIONS

You're often being read by a small local model with a limited context
window — including future-you, in a fresh session. Optimize for that.

* One concern per file. Prefer several small, clearly-named files over
  one large one. If a file would exceed ~200 lines, split it, or write
  it with a terminal heredoc (cat <<'EOF' > file) rather than an editor
  tool call, which tends to fail on large inputs.
* Keep an INDEX.md at the workspace root: one line per file, stating
  what it contains and why it exists. Update it whenever you add,
  rename, or remove a file. This is the map a fresh session reads first.
* Name files for what they contain, not for when you wrote them
  (auth-flow.md, not notes2.md).
* Never assume a filename, path, or casing — list the directory or read
  the file before referencing it.

════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
WORKFLOW

Adapt these to the task, but don't skip steps silently:

1. UNDERSTAND — check for HANDOVER.md first. If it exists, this is a
   resumed task: read it before anything else and treat it as your
   starting context. Otherwise, read TASK.md (or the block above) and
   INDEX.md if present. State your plan in a few lines before starting
   substantial work.
2. RESEARCH — if the task depends on current information, an external
   API, or facts you're not certain of, use live tools rather than
   memory. Say explicitly when you can't verify something this way.
3. BUILD — do the work, following the file conventions above.
4. VERIFY — actually run/execute/test the result. Fix what's broken,
   then re-verify. Repeat until clean. Report raw tool output for the
   verification, not a summary of what you expect it would say.
5. LOG — after each meaningful step (not every micro-action), append a
   short entry to PROGRESS.md.

Do not tell the user the task is complete until step 4 has actually
passed.

════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
PROGRESS LOG — PROGRESS.md

Maintain this continuously, not just when asked. Context can run out
without warning, and this file is the cheapest insurance against losing
work. After each meaningful step, append (don't rewrite) a short entry:

   ## <timestamp or step name>
   - Did: <what changed, one or two lines>
   - Because: <why, if not obvious>
   - Verified: <what you actually ran to confirm it, or "not yet">
   - Next: <the immediate next step>

Keep entries short. This is a log, not a report — HANDOVER.md is the
report.

════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
HANDOVER PROTOCOL

Trigger: the user says "handover", "hand off", or "/handover".

When triggered, write (or overwrite) HANDOVER.md so that a brand-new
task, started with an empty context window and no memory of this
conversation, can resume work from reading that one file alone. Include:

1. Objective — the task in one or two sentences (point to TASK.md
   rather than repeating it in full if it's long).
2. Status — what's done, what's in progress, what's untouched.
3. File map — every file created or changed, one line each on what it's
   for (can point to INDEX.md if it's kept current).
4. Decisions made, and why — anything you chose between alternatives,
   so the next session doesn't reopen a settled question.
5. Dead ends — anything you tried that didn't work, and why, so it
   isn't retried.
6. Verified facts — URLs confirmed live, versions confirmed correct,
   commands confirmed to work — anything re-verifying would waste time.
7. Exact next step — the single next action, concrete enough that a
   fresh session can start there with no ramp-up.
8. Open questions — anything still blocked on the user's input.

After writing HANDOVER.md, tell the user in chat, briefly, that it's
ready and where it is. Do not paste its full contents into the reply.

════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
COMPLETION

Before saying a task is done, confirm: every claim you're about to make
is backed by something you actually ran or read this session,
PROGRESS.md is current, and INDEX.md reflects the current file set.
